1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,666
Normalization puts each fact in one place.

2
00:00:03,666 --> 00:00:09,861
Denormalization copies a fact on purpose because a read path is too expensive without it.

3
00:00:09,861 --> 00:00:11,833
That purpose has to be named.

4
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:15,291
A copy without an owner is just a bug you scheduled.

5
00:00:15,291 --> 00:00:18,694
Example: orders.customer_city.

6
00:00:18,694 --> 00:00:21,000
You already have city on customers.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,930
You copy it because a nightly report cannot afford the join.

8
00:00:24,930 --> 00:00:32,652
Now a move to Denver must update customers and every open order, or you accept stale city on old orders.

9
00:00:32,652 --> 00:00:34,333
Those are different products.

10
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:38,194
A snapshot on a closed order can be a fact about history.

11
00:00:38,194 --> 00:00:41,486
A living copy of the current city is a cache.

12
00:00:41,486 --> 00:00:46,847
Caches need a rule: who writes, when, and what happens on a miss.

13
00:00:46,847 --> 00:00:49,888
Summary tables are denormalization too.

14
00:00:49,888 --> 00:00:54,194
daily_sales holds a date and a SUM you could recompute.

15
00:00:54,194 --> 00:00:57,708
You keep it because the recompute is overnight work.

16
00:00:57,708 --> 00:00:59,541
You refresh it on a schedule.

17
00:00:59,541 --> 00:01:04,041
If the summary disagrees with the base table, the base table wins.

18
00:01:04,041 --> 00:01:05,208
Write that down.

19
00:01:05,208 --> 00:01:10,736
Do not denormalize during the first sketch because a mockup showed city next to total.

20
00:01:10,736 --> 00:01:12,111
Screens can join.

21
00:01:12,111 --> 00:01:13,291
Start honest.

22
00:01:13,291 --> 00:01:19,000
Copy when a measured query is too slow, or when history must freeze a value.

23
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,555
Indexes are not denormalization.

24
00:01:21,555 --> 00:01:24,458
They copy keys, not business facts.

25
00:01:24,458 --> 00:01:25,777
Add them first.

26
00:01:25,777 --> 00:01:29,111
When you do copy a fact, constrain what you can.

27
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:31,666
A trigger might keep the copy in sync.

28
00:01:31,666 --> 00:01:34,611
Triggers are easy to forget during a bulk load.

29
00:01:34,611 --> 00:01:37,708
An application service might be the only writer.

30
00:01:37,708 --> 00:01:39,819
Then two writers will drift.

31
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:41,375
Name the single writer.

32
00:01:41,375 --> 00:01:47,375
On a diagram, mark copied columns so nobody thinks city is born on orders.

33
00:01:47,375 --> 00:01:50,222
The lesson is not that copies are forbidden.

34
00:01:50,222 --> 00:01:57,305
The lesson is that copies are a product decision with an owner, a refresh rule, and a source of truth.

35
00:01:57,305 --> 00:02:01,597
Materialized views are a managed summary table in some engines.

36
00:02:01,597 --> 00:02:03,430
They still have a refresh rule.

37
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:06,000
They still lose a fight with the base table.

38
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,888
Search engines and caches outside the database are denormalization too.

39
00:02:10,888 --> 00:02:15,361
The owner might be a worker that rebuilds a document on every order.

40
00:02:15,361 --> 00:02:19,750
If the worker is down, the product should say stale, not true.

41
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:22,138
That product sentence is the design.

42
00:02:22,138 --> 00:02:26,805
Copying a price onto an order line at purchase time is a snapshot.

43
00:02:26,805 --> 00:02:29,416
The catalog price may change tomorrow.

44
00:02:29,416 --> 00:02:30,861
The line must not.

45
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:33,847
That copy is not a cache of the catalog.

46
00:02:33,847 --> 00:02:35,500
It is the price you agreed.

47
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:41,708
Name it unit_price_at_sale so nobody 'fixes' it from the catalog.

48
00:02:41,708 --> 00:02:44,222
Names prevent helpful disasters.

49
00:02:44,222 --> 00:02:48,680
Read-time joins versus write-time copies is the real spectrum.

50
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:50,166
Start at read-time.

51
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:53,875
Move left only with a measurement or a legal freeze.

52
00:02:53,875 --> 00:02:57,263
A legal freeze is a measurement of a different kind.

53
00:02:57,263 --> 00:02:59,958
It still has an owner: the invoice.

54
00:02:59,958 --> 00:03:04,069
Do not denormalize because an ORM is awkward to join.

55
00:03:04,069 --> 00:03:05,722
Fix the query helper.

56
00:03:05,722 --> 00:03:07,527
Awkward is not slow.

57
00:03:07,527 --> 00:03:08,791
Slow is slow.

58
00:03:08,791 --> 00:03:15,777
The copy is a product decision with an owner, a refresh or freeze rule, and a source of truth.

59
00:03:15,777 --> 00:03:17,430
Say those three out loud.

60
00:03:17,430 --> 00:03:20,750
If you cannot, you are not denormalizing yet.

61
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:22,138
You are duplicating.

62
00:03:22,138 --> 00:03:26,847
Duplication is how Austin and Denver both stay true for Maya.

63
00:03:26,847 --> 00:03:29,208
We already decided that is not a goal.

64
00:03:29,208 --> 00:03:31,291
On purpose, or not at all.

65
00:03:31,291 --> 00:03:34,388
If a copy has no owner, delete the column.

66
00:03:34,388 --> 00:03:38,194
If it has an owner, write the refresh in the same migration.

67
00:03:38,194 --> 00:03:41,180
Future dashboards will ask for another copy.

68
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:44,472
Hand them the three-sentence test before you say yes.

69
00:03:44,472 --> 00:03:46,402
A copy is a second clock.

70
00:03:46,402 --> 00:03:47,930
Two clocks disagree.

71
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:51,875
You must say which clock is for display and which is for truth.

72
00:03:51,875 --> 00:03:55,541
Truth stays normalized until a snapshot is the product.

73
00:03:55,541 --> 00:03:58,333
An invoice line price is a snapshot.

74
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:02,194
A cached city on an open cart is a clock that will lie.

75
00:04:02,194 --> 00:04:06,069
Do not ship the liar unless a worker owns the rewind.

76
00:04:06,069 --> 00:04:08,638
Name the worker in the migration comment.

77
00:04:08,638 --> 00:04:12,208
If you cannot name the worker, you are not ready to copy.

78
00:04:12,208 --> 00:04:13,694
Join a little longer.

79
00:04:13,694 --> 00:04:16,277
Indexes are cheaper than a second clock.

80
00:04:16,277 --> 00:04:21,513
When you finally copy, freeze or refresh, never both in silence.

81
00:04:21,513 --> 00:04:23,486
Silence is duplication.

82
00:04:23,486 --> 00:04:25,652
Speech is denormalization.

83
00:04:25,652 --> 00:04:26,861
Use speech.

84
00:04:26,861 --> 00:04:31,180
Normalization splits tables so each fact has one home.

85
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:36,638
Denormalization copies a fact on purpose so a frequent query can avoid a join.

86
00:04:36,638 --> 00:04:42,263
It is a last-ditch optimization after you have evidence, not a shortcut past design.

87
00:04:42,386 --> 00:04:47,461
A clean schema can still make a dashboard join five tables and take seconds.

88
00:04:47,461 --> 00:04:50,669
Users feel the seconds, not the purity.

89
00:04:50,669 --> 00:04:53,544
The responsible version has two steps.

90
00:04:53,544 --> 00:04:59,099
First, watch which queries run often and which of them join more than two tables.

91
00:04:59,099 --> 00:05:01,863
Guessing is how you duplicate the wrong column.

92
00:05:01,863 --> 00:05:09,558
Second, copy a column into a table that already participates, so the hot query joins less, or not at all.

93
00:05:09,558 --> 00:05:20,919
A music catalog might store artist_name on a track row that already has album_id, if top tracks by famous artists are the hot path and always needed the name.

94
00:05:20,919 --> 00:05:28,780
You now update artist_name in two places, or you accept a nightly rebuild, or you use a trigger to copy.

95
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:30,947
That extra write is the cost.

96
00:05:30,947 --> 00:05:34,016
The extra consistency work is the cost.

97
00:05:34,016 --> 00:05:38,988
If you cannot name the job that keeps the copy true, you did not denormalize.

98
00:05:38,988 --> 00:05:42,044
You created an anomaly and called it speed.

99
00:05:42,044 --> 00:05:49,822
Document the source of truth: artists.name is canonical, tracks.artist_name is a cache.

100
00:05:49,822 --> 00:05:54,474
If they disagree, the canonical wins, and the cache is rebuilt.

101
00:05:54,474 --> 00:05:57,502
Do not denormalize because joins look scary.

102
00:05:57,502 --> 00:06:01,724
Joins on keys with indexes are the relational model working.

103
00:06:01,724 --> 00:06:10,224
Denormalize because a measured query is hot, the join is the bottleneck, and the copied column is stable enough to cache.

104
00:06:10,224 --> 00:06:15,155
Counts and totals on an order header copied from lines are a common form.

105
00:06:15,155 --> 00:06:23,627
They go stale the moment a line changes unless a trigger or application transaction updates the header in the same unit of work.

106
00:06:23,627 --> 00:06:27,169
If that unit of work is missing, the header is a rumor.

107
00:06:27,169 --> 00:06:34,252
Sometimes a reporting table filled on a schedule is cleaner than sprinkling copies through operational tables.

108
00:06:34,252 --> 00:06:36,391
That is still denormalization.

109
00:06:36,391 --> 00:06:42,683
It keeps the OLTP schema honest and puts redundancy in a place you already rebuild.

110
00:06:42,683 --> 00:06:46,419
The lesson after weeks of normal forms is restraint.

111
00:06:46,419 --> 00:06:48,988
You now know why redundancy hurts.

112
00:06:48,988 --> 00:06:51,558
So when you add it back, you add a broom.

113
00:06:51,558 --> 00:06:53,586
No broom, no copy.

114
00:06:53,586 --> 00:06:56,308
Speed is not a reason to forget the broom.

115
00:06:56,308 --> 00:07:02,447
Hot query: top tracks for an artist, joining artists to albums to tracks every time.

116
00:07:02,447 --> 00:07:09,127
Evidence: it is the home page, it runs constantly, EXPLAIN shows the joins as the cost.

117
00:07:09,127 --> 00:07:19,016
Copy artist_id and artist_name onto tracks, or keep artist_id only if you still join artists, which you were trying not to.

118
00:07:19,016 --> 00:07:24,363
If you copy the name, a trigger on artists UPDATE writes the new name to tracks.

119
00:07:24,363 --> 00:07:28,363
If you skip the trigger, the home page lies after a rebrand.

120
00:07:28,363 --> 00:07:35,113
A nightly table home_top_tracks rebuilt from the normalized sources may be cleaner.

121
00:07:35,113 --> 00:07:36,891
Readers hit the snapshot.

122
00:07:36,891 --> 00:07:39,252
Writers hit the normalized tables.

123
00:07:39,252 --> 00:07:43,377
That is denormalization with a broom called the rebuild job.

124
00:07:43,377 --> 00:07:46,544
Without the job, you only added redundancy.

125
00:07:46,544 --> 00:07:52,516
With the job, you bought seconds for users and kept a place that is allowed to be redundant.

126
00:07:52,516 --> 00:08:00,183
That is the twist after learning to split tables: sometimes you add a snapshot, on purpose, with a name that admits it.

127
00:08:00,183 --> 00:08:03,822
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

128
00:08:03,822 --> 00:08:06,877
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

129
00:08:06,877 --> 00:08:08,586
What is denormalization?

130
00:08:08,586 --> 00:08:12,572
Copying a fact on purpose after evidence, with a broom.

131
00:08:12,572 --> 00:08:13,697
Without a broom?

132
00:08:13,697 --> 00:08:15,669
An anomaly named speed.

133
00:08:15,669 --> 00:08:16,377
When?

134
00:08:16,377 --> 00:08:22,391
A hot query, measured, joins are the cost, column stable enough to cache.

135
00:08:22,391 --> 00:08:23,683
Source of truth?

136
00:08:23,683 --> 00:08:24,766
Document it.

137
00:08:24,766 --> 00:08:26,808
Cache loses if they disagree.

138
00:08:26,808 --> 00:08:28,086
Reporting table?

139
00:08:28,086 --> 00:08:32,349
Often cleaner than sprinkling copies through operational tables.

140
00:08:32,349 --> 00:08:34,183
Trigger or transaction?

141
00:08:34,183 --> 00:08:36,586
How an order header total stays true.

142
00:08:36,586 --> 00:08:37,780
Joins scary?

143
00:08:37,780 --> 00:08:38,849
Not a reason.

144
00:08:38,849 --> 00:08:41,711
Keys plus indexes are the model working.

145
00:08:41,711 --> 00:08:43,197
Home page tracks?

146
00:08:43,197 --> 00:08:47,058
Snapshot table rebuilt from normalized sources.

147
00:08:47,058 --> 00:08:48,419
Name the snapshot?

148
00:08:48,419 --> 00:08:51,294
So nobody treats it as the only truth.

149
00:08:51,294 --> 00:08:52,308
Restraint?

150
00:08:52,308 --> 00:08:57,183
You know why redundancy hurts, so you add a broom when you add it back.

151
00:08:57,183 --> 00:09:01,905
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

